WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.155 --> 00:00:04.759
On your screen right
now are thousands of ancient galaxies...

00:00:05.126 --> 00:00:07.929
brought to you in one of the first
five images

00:00:07.929 --> 00:00:11.745
taken by the James
Webb Space Telescope or JWST.

00:00:12.714 --> 00:00:15.750
The region captured here spans
as much of the sky

00:00:15.750 --> 00:00:19.138
as a grain of sand would cover,
if held at arm's length.

00:00:19.773 --> 00:00:24.068
The farther away these galaxies are,
the longer their light took to reach us.

00:00:24.497 --> 00:00:29.435
This view reaches back
more than 13 billion years into the past.

00:00:30.067 --> 00:00:33.670
But JWST isn't just witnessing history.

00:00:33.801 --> 00:00:35.526
It's making it.

00:00:35.636 --> 00:00:41.341
Launched in 2021 after more than 25 years
of planning, the space telescope traveled

00:00:41.341 --> 00:00:44.965
1.5 million kilometers away from Earth

00:00:45.491 --> 00:00:47.906
to give us a new view of the universe.

00:00:48.264 --> 00:00:50.766
This is the Carina Nebula.

00:00:50.930 --> 00:00:54.100
JWST can see deep into its clouds

00:00:54.100 --> 00:00:57.023
and reveal stars as they form.

00:00:57.458 --> 00:01:00.381
Our sun was actually born in a cloud
like this.

00:01:01.495 --> 00:01:04.349
Here we see the opposite
end of a star's life cycle

00:01:04.647 --> 00:01:06.444
through the Southern Ring Nebula.

00:01:06.829 --> 00:01:09.732
At its center,
a star has run out of fuel and partially

00:01:09.732 --> 00:01:12.804
exploded,
leaving behind beautiful ripples.

00:01:13.307 --> 00:01:17.511
These ripples are one of the main ways
that elements like carbon, an important

00:01:17.511 --> 00:01:21.697
building block for planets and life,
get spread throughout the universe.

00:01:22.646 --> 00:01:26.283
Next is a group of galaxies
called Stephan's Quintet.

00:01:26.776 --> 00:01:29.579
As these galaxies fall
closer to each other,

00:01:29.812 --> 00:01:33.349
they're starting to swirl, like water
going down a drain.

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:39.072
With JWST, we can see thousands
of other galaxies in the background too.

00:01:39.935 --> 00:01:44.706
Lastly, JWST measured light from a planet
called WASP 96b

00:01:45.073 --> 00:01:47.142
orbiting another star in the Milky Way.

00:01:47.585 --> 00:01:49.888
The signal shows
that this particular planet,

00:01:50.054 --> 00:01:52.757
a gas giant like Jupiter, has clouds.

00:01:53.562 --> 00:01:57.133
The telescope will look
for similar signals from rocky planets

00:01:57.297 --> 00:01:59.365
that could potentially host life.

00:01:59.687 --> 00:02:02.624
These images are just a glimpse of
what's to come.

00:02:03.217 --> 00:02:06.520
JWST did all of this in a matter of days.

00:02:06.969 --> 00:02:09.605
It has enough fuel for 20 years.

